  "primary_subject_agency_ids": [],
  "summary": "ACLU's Jay Stanley surveys ALPR vendors beyond Flock \u2014 including Motorola/Vigilant, Axon, Rekor, and Leonardo/ELSAG \u2014 arguing that switching vendors won't fix civil-liberties problems absent legal limits on retention, sharing, AI analytics, and federal access. The piece compares vendors on nationwide sharing, retention, federal/ICE access, cloud vs. local storage, AI search, suspicion generation, and NCIC use.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "simply replacing Flock surveillance with another company's surveillance won't necessarily solve any of the privacy and other civil liberties problems posed by this mass surveillance technology.",
      "paragraph_idx": 6
    },
    {
      "quote": "Motorola, however, has a contract with ICE (through Thompson-Reuters) permitting that agency to directly search Motorola's surveillance database",
      "paragraph_idx": 24
    },
    {
      "quote": "When Denver decided to discontinue its contract with Flock earlier this year, it (hastily and unwisely) switched to a new vendor (Axon).",
      "paragraph_idx": 30
    },
    {
      "quote": "One provider, Rekor, provides ALPR services to the Westchester County, NY police, which is the subject of a class action lawsuit",
      "paragraph_idx": 16
    }
  ],
